# Every text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every machine.
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf on rewrote whole files as
# CRLF, a commit of one changed line showed thousands of changed lines, and
# the tests that read these files saw `\r\n` on Linux. This line has to come
# first: the rules below override it for the files that must stay as they are.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The Windows launcher is run by `cmd`, which mis-parses a batch file whose
# lines end with LF alone — a line can end mid-token and the file silently does
# the wrong thing. `-text` keeps the CRLF the bundler writes, on checkout and
# on commit, whatever the user's core.autocrlf says.
*.cmd -text
*.bat -text

# The packed runner is binary; never let a line-ending filter near it.
*.tgz binary
